  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Assuming that $n$ is an integer, the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nx={\\theta }_{1}+bn,\\,x={\\theta }_{2}+bn,\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2}.$ Find the values of ${\\theta }_{1}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}$ in $[0,2\\pi ),$ and the value of $b.$\n\n${\\theta }_{1}=${{field-1}}$,\\,{\\theta }_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,b=$\n{{field-3}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}\u2061(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $4$th quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/5af1febb2c8dbf1b19ee64addd8f8ccdd5a30f9330d36ab800475fce9f6740c2.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution for the given equation is \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nWriting this as $x=\\theta +2n\\pi ,$ where $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ we get\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ we have ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4},$ ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{7\\pi }{4},$ and $b=2\\pi .$",
